## **Overview**

**What it is**

Grocery Pal is a mobile app that helps individual shoppers track their grocery buys by expiry dates, so as to minimise food waste.

**The problem**

I regularly forgot what groceries I already had, overbought items, and discovered expired food too late. Existing pantry apps either created too much administrative work or hid key features behind subscriptions.

I wanted to build an app I could use to keep track of my own food inventory.

**My role**

I was the sole product designer for this project, and used Claude Code as a co-working partner to implement the development.

**Timeline**

I worked on this app for roughly a week (early-mid July 2026), scoped as MVP.

**Tools**

Figma, Claude Code

## Opportunity

**Why not existing apps?**

I looked at existing grocery management apps - such as KitchenPal, Pantry Pal, Stock Pal, but found them to not meet my needs. For some, the UI wasn’t clean or minimalistic, and required a learning curve to use the app. Some did not have AI receipt-scanning capabilities, or gated it behind a paywall.

I wanted to design and ship an MVP quickly, and Claude Code enabled that.

Having done preliminary research into this problem space during a Build For Good Hackathon in 2024, I realized that this was an interesting and rich problem space, and thus wanted to try my hand at designing for it.

## Goals

**Project goals**

- Ship a working, interactive MVP
- Explore an AI-assisted product design and implementation workflow.

**User goals**

- Add or remove items easily from inventory with minimal friction
- Make administrative workload of managing food inventory enjoyable
- Make calm, informed decisions about groceries

## Research Insights

From prior research done with 25 participants in a user testing session with a previous iteration of a grocery management app (KiasuKitchen), these were some key research findings:

- Manual logging/editing is the top friction point and adoption risk. Participants found it tiring to scan receipts, and won’t want to have to update the app constantly.
- Users don't know or want to type exact expiry dates. Users are unsure of exact expiry dates, and want the app to auto populate them.
- Cooked food/leftovers are a bigger waste source than groceries for several households.

## Design Principles

**Core UX principles**

Here are some principles I kept in mind as I worked through the designs:

- Minimize friction for stock upload or deletion. I learned through prior research that tedious processes of managing stock was the number one reasons users would be put off from using the app. As a user myself, I heavily empathized with this.
- Maximize visibility into current stock. Not being able to see what was bought, thus forgetting about them was a high reason for food wastage.
- Maximize joy. The task of maintaining a grocery inventory is already a tedious task, thus a well-designed product can make the experience more enjoyable and satisfying.

**Visual direction**

Muted and calm tones, with pops of colour at key moments were chosen to create an experience that feels calm, organised, and enjoyable, rather than anxiety inducing. Green was chosen as a primary brand colour, to convey the freshness users will be able to enjoy with regular stock management. A clean and minimal UI helps users scan through the interface quickly, and focus on key actions they want to take, reducing friction in the tasks they want to carry out. WCAG 2 was adhered to, creating an accessible and user-friendly experience.

**Motion**

The goal is for motion to be calm, purposeful, and invisible, helping the app feel more polished. For instance, an editable card that expands in place and scrolls to the top of the screen helps to anchor users in their current flows without losing context of where they were along the items they need to review. Claude Code was used to add motion to the developed UI, through prompting and iterations.

### AI-Assisted Design Workflow

**Rapid concept generation**

I used Claude design to do up first draft designs of the flows/screens I wanted to work on first, such as the Home screen/adding items flow. It was able to quickly create a working prototype, so I could start interacting with it on my device, and also start thinking more deeply about how interactions should be. Once a flow was validated in code, I continued to work in Figma on refining the design - colours, spacing, hierachy etc. At this point, matching tokens between Figma and Claude Code was not a priority.

