### Challenges

**Product Challenges**

Seeking the right balance between highlighting elements using brighter colours/chips was something I wanted to do well. Too much would cause the interface to look cluttered, and too little would defeat the purpose of the app. I made a design decision to remove the expiry notification chips from each card, and only use them to highlight food items that needed immediate attention.

Another challenge was balancing how much control to give users in adjusting AI generated values. AI will generate Item name, Category, Item Quantity, Quantity units, Expiry date whenever a user uploads an item. Using a date picker might have allowed users to choose an exact expiry date, but from user research and personal experience, it wasn’t very often that people are able to give an exact expiry date. Thus, a stepper component was used for both Expiry dates and Item quantities.

**AI Workflow**

I had to learn better techniques for prompting in order to manage my tokens better. I frequently ran out of tokens, and learned about techniques of choosing models, starting new sessions for new tasks, compacting sessions in order to better utilise my tokens.

There are a lot of resources to learn on how to get started using AI, that it might be tempted to keep ingesting resources, but once you get started, there are specific questions you might be interested to learn more about, and it’s easier to go from there. From example, wanting to learn how I can get Claude Code to generate designs based on my current designs led me to learn about DESIGN.md and how to properly structure it.

### Final Product

Grocery Pal is a working MVP deployed via Vercel, connected to Claude API, able to scan receipts and log them as part of a grocery management system. I was able to quickly iterate and ship my designs in 1 week as part of this experiment to learn about using Claude Code, and it has only increased my appetite to learn more about how I can better use it.

Some of its current features include:

- Receipt scanning
- AI categorisation
- Expiry tracking
- Editing
- Inventory management

A list of backlogged items and features are awaiting further work and development.

### Reflections

**As a product designer**

Working directly in code with AI changed how I evaluate my own design decisions. A tradeoff between complexity in build and nice to have features became more real because I had to manage the cost of it directly, which made me more honest about ‘nice to have’ ideas, and also pare down on refining the most important parts of the app.

**Working with AI tools**

Incorporating AI into my design flow at specific points seems to offer benefits that I would want to take more advantage of. AI can help in quickly exploring different initial directions, doing up a first draft of a screen or flow that undergoes further refinement, and also for prototyping. It’s a fast way to get past a blank canvas and into iterating on your designs, so that designers can take over to get the designs to become ready for development.
